{{also|suden}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etym|gml|de}} and {{etym|dum|de}} {{term|sūden|lang=gml}}. The form is already attested in {{etym|gmh|-}} {{term|sūden|lang=gmh}}, which replaces native Middle High German {{term|sunden|lang=gml}} from {{etym|goh|-}} {{term|sundan|lang=de}}. However, the modern form with a preserved monophthong must be directly from Middle Low German or Middle Dutch. The latter's influence is seen in the umlaut (cf. the Middle Dutch pronunciation of ''-ū-'' [yː]). All these forms are from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*sunþanē}}, an adverb meaning “from the south”. The original Proto-Germanic noun {{m|gem-pro|*sunþrą}}, whence English {{term|south|lang=en}} and German {{term|Süd|lang=de}}, has always been restricted chiefly to compounds in continental West Germanic.

